$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General/Typical Procedure: 3. General procedure of catalytic asymmetric
Grignard reagents to aldehydes. In flask A, 126.5 mg (0.445 mmol)
Ti(OiPr)4 was added dropwise into a solution of 21.5 mg (0.075 mmol)
(S)-BINOL in 2.0 mL dry MTBE under argon atmosphere at ambient
temperature and was stirred 0.5 h. In flask B, 1.08 ml (1.0 mmol, 0.926 M
in THF) i BuMgBr was slowly added into 160.3 mg (1.0 mmol) BDMAEE in 2.0
mL dry MTBE in an ice-water bath under argon within 5 min and then was
stirred for 30 min. The mixture A was then introduced into the mixture B
in an ice-water bath. After naturally warmed to ambient temperature, the
combined milk yellow mixture was stirred for 1.0 h. Then it was cooled
with an ice-salt bath, 53.1 mg (0.5 mmol) benzaldehyde was added
dropwise. The mixture was naturally warmed to ambient temperature and
stirred until benzaldehyde could not be checked by TLC. The reaction was
quenched by 5% cold aqueous HCl solution and extracted with ether three
times. The combined organic layers were dried with anhydrous Na2SO4 and
evaporated in vacuo to give a pale yellow oil. Preparative TLC gave yield
76%. The ee was determined as 98.0% with chiral HPLC. The absolute
configuration of the alcohol was assigned to be S by comparison of the
optical rotation ([α]D 20= -25° (c 2.5, EtOH)) with the reported data.6
(S,E)-5-methyl-1-phenylhex-1-en-3-ol (21). [α]D 20 = +15.4° (c 2.61,
EtOH). 1H NMR (200 MHz) δ 7.23-7.36 (m, 5H), 6.58 (d, 1H, J = 15.9 Hz),
6.16-6.27 (dd, 1H, J = 6.8 Hz, J = 15.9 Hz), 4.36 (m, 1H), 1.39-1.82 (m,
4H), 0.96 (d, 3H, J = 6.4 Hz), 0.96 (d, 3H, J = 6.6 Hz). The ee value was
determined by chiral HPLC with Daicel chiral OD-H column. Eluent: hexane/
i PrOH = 90/10, flow rate: 1.0 mL/min, retention time: major peak 5.75
min, minor peak 6.49 min.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective, reaction mixture from stage 1 added to reaction mixture
from stage 2 in stage 3, Grignard reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Highly Catalytic Asymmetric Addition of Deactivated Alkyl Grignard
Reagents to Aldehydes
